57. Methods of Primary Data Collection
Paper: Data Collection and Analysis for Social Science Research (ECNAEC301) | Unit: I | Topic: Methods of data collection – primary and secondary data; observation, interview, questionnaire, schedules
Introduction
Research relies on data. Primary data are collected first-hand by the researcher for the specific purpose of the study. They are original and tailored, but time-consuming and costly to gather. Secondary data are covered in the next essay.
Observation method
The researcher systematically watches and records behaviour. Types: structured (predetermined categories) versus unstructured; participant (researcher joins the group) versus non-participant; controlled (laboratory) versus uncontrolled (natural setting); disguised or undisguised.
• Merits: direct, records actual behaviour, no respondent dependence, useful for those who cannot report (children).
• Demerits: time-consuming, observer bias, limited to observable behaviour, cannot study attitudes or past events, ethical issues.
Interview method
Face-to-face or telephone oral questioning. Types: structured (fixed questions), unstructured (free-flowing), semi-structured, focused, personal versus group (focus group discussions), depth interviews.
• Merits: high response, flexibility, probing, can include illiterate respondents, observe non-verbal cues.
• Demerits: costly, time-consuming, interviewer bias, difficult for scattered populations, respondent reluctance.
Questionnaire
A list of questions sent to respondents, who fill it in themselves (mailed, online, or hand-delivered). Types of questions: open-ended and closed (dichotomous, multiple choice, rating scales such as Likert).
Good questionnaire: clear and simple language, logical order, no leading or double-barrelled questions, pretesting (pilot), covering letter, appropriate length.
• Merits: economical, wide coverage, respondent convenience and anonymity, no interviewer bias.
• Demerits: low response, needs literacy, no probing, possible misunderstanding, incomplete answers.
Schedules
A schedule is a proforma that is filled in by an enumerator while interviewing the respondent. It is used where respondents are illiterate (rural surveys, Census).
Difference: questionnaire is mailed and self-filled; schedule is filled by enumerator. Schedule has higher response and accuracy but higher cost and requires trained enumerators; the questionnaire is cheaper but with non-response problems.
Choosing a method
Depends on research objective, nature of population, literacy, budget, time and accuracy required. Often mixed methods are used.
Conclusion
Each primary method has strengths and limits; careful design, pretesting and trained field staff ensure reliable data.